  "constitution.learn.subtitle": "8 lessons — one per slider. Theory + historical example + hands-on task. After 8/8 — a PDF certificate.",
  "constitution.learn.task_heading": "🎯 Task",
  "constitution.learn.theory_heading": "Theory",
  "constitution.learn.title": "AEVION Constitution Academy",
  "constitution.lesson.floor.example": "1945–1980 Western Europe. Free universities in Germany and France, universal healthcare in Britain (NHS 1948), public housing in Sweden. Floor grew from 20 to 70 in one generation — and capitalism gained mass legitimacy for the first time.",
  "constitution.lesson.floor.hint": "Raise floor ≥70 — you'll get the Nordic model.",
  "constitution.lesson.floor.theory": "The 20th-century discovery: the elite stops fearing the bottom not when the bottom is disarmed, but when the bottom has something to lose. The floor below is a guaranteed minimum — education, basic healthcare, pensions, sometimes UBI. When people aren't pressed against the wall, they have no existential motivation to take. This is the most expensive and most stabilizing change you can make to a society — but it requires a growing pie.",
  "constitution.lesson.floor.title": "Floor below",
  "constitution.lesson.multiStatus.example": "Japan: respect for the craftsman (shokunin) persisted alongside respect for the corporate manager. A school teacher in Finland has more prestige than a lawyer. In both cases this isn't natural — it's centuries of cultural work.",
  "constitution.lesson.multiStatus.hint": "Raise multi-status ≥70 — assemble all 4 pillars → Open Access Order.",
  "constitution.lesson.multiStatus.theory": "When respect can only be reached on one axis (say, money), all of society lines up and fights for it. When there are many legitimate axes — science, craft, care, art, sport — ambitions disperse, competition becomes less toxic. This is the most fragile change because you can't legislate it — it grows from culture.",
  "constitution.lesson.multiStatus.title": "Multiple status axes",
  "constitution.lesson.polycentricity.example": "Switzerland: 26 cantons, each with its own tax regime and education system. Zug (low tax, corporations) vs Basel (high tax, pharma cluster) — compete for residents. Who disagrees moves in 2 hours by train. That's polycentricity in action.",
  "constitution.lesson.polycentricity.hint": "Raise polycentricity ≥75 — you'll get Network Post-Nation (competing jurisdictions).",
  "constitution.lesson.polycentricity.theory": "Elinor Ostrom (2009 Nobel) showed: common resources are best managed not by centralized state and not by privatization, but by polycentric communities with real autonomy. Swiss cantons, Indian village waterworks, North American coastal fishing cooperatives — all work through local rules + right to exit.",
  "constitution.lesson.polycentricity.title": "Polycentricity",
  "constitution.lesson.positiveSum.example": "Venezuela 2000s: high oil prices → generous social programs (floor grew from 30 to 60). After the price crash (2014–) positiveSum collapsed, floor didn't hold, the country fell into hyperinflation. Without a growing economy a high floor is an illusion.",
  "constitution.lesson.positiveSum.hint": "Raise positive sum ≥65 with other sliders low — you'll get Extractive Boom (growth captured by a narrow group).",
  "constitution.lesson.positiveSum.theory": "Without a growing pie all other sliders slip backward. Feudalism lasted 800 years precisely because positiveSum was near zero — the economy didn't grow, so if someone got more, someone got less. The Industrial Revolution (1750–1850) gave the first long stretch of positiveSum growth, which made everything afterward possible: suffrage, social state, open access. Any constitutional reform either rides growth or is doomed.",
  "constitution.lesson.positiveSum.title": "Positive sum",
  "constitution.lesson.rotation.example": "Ireland 2016: 99 random citizens deliberated 18 months on abortion. Their recommendation became the basis of the 2018 referendum — abortion rights passed with 66%. Lottery solved a question parliament couldn't move for 30 years.",
  "constitution.lesson.rotation.hint": "Raise rotation ≥65 — you'll get Ancient Polis (but careful: high rotation + low multi-status = exclusion).",
  "constitution.lesson.rotation.theory": "Athens 5th c. BCE: half of government positions were filled by lot. It wasn't 'random' — it was protection against permanent coalitions forming. When the top rotates, those below have a path up, and the top has less fear of being a permanent target. Modern sortition (Swiss referendums, Irish Citizens' Assembly) brings the Athenian principle back to the 21st century.",
  "constitution.lesson.rotation.title": "Rotation / sortition",
  "constitution.lesson.ruleOfLaw.example": "Singapore and 19th-century UK — two paths to high ruleOfLaw. UK: evolutionary through parliament. Singapore: tough top-down under Lee Kuan Yew. Both reduce corruption, but the second leaves no rotation, giving fragility on succession crisis.",
  "constitution.lesson.ruleOfLaw.hint": "Drop ruleOfLaw ≤35 — you'll get Late Feudalism (law respects form, not substance).",
  "constitution.lesson.ruleOfLaw.theory": "Magna Carta 1215 — the first recorded case of a king agreeing to live by written rules. Today obvious; 800 years ago revolutionary. The higher ruleOfLaw, the less elites fight each other: the loser knows they lost by the rules, not because they were cheated. This reduces intrigue intensity and turns competition into a productive channel.",
  "constitution.lesson.ruleOfLaw.title": "Rule of law binds the top too",
  "constitution.lesson.skinInGame.example": "2008 financial crisis — Lehman Brothers traders got bonuses for 2005-2007, then the firm collapsed, but their salaries stayed. Since 2010 the EU has clawback rules: bonuses can be recalled in 5–7 years if deals turn out destructive.",
  "constitution.lesson.skinInGame.hint": "Raise skin ≥85 — you'll get 'Nomadic Steppe' (may classify as mixed without right weights on other sliders).",
  "constitution.lesson.skinInGame.theory": "Taleb: 'Whoever makes a decision must bear its consequences personally.' When a banker risks only salary and depositors risk money, the banker takes asymmetric risks. Nomadic steppe is an example of extreme skin-in-game: a chief who loses a battle leading it himself loses his head or his tribe. Low skin-in-game → risk transfer → systemic crisis.",
  "constitution.lesson.skinInGame.title": "Skin in the game",
  "constitution.lesson.transparency.example": "Estonia — every citizen can see who and when accessed their personal data in state systems. This reverse transparency ('you watch me → I watch you') built trust in e-government faster than anywhere in Europe.",
  "constitution.lesson.transparency.hint": "Drop transparency ≤25 — you'll get Authoritarian Vertical (dark rooms + anxious top).",
  "constitution.lesson.transparency.theory": "The cheapest constitutional upgrade — public official-income declarations, open budgets, access to decision data. Transparency doesn't kill power, it kills paranoia from below. The Swedish 'principle of public access' (1766 — oldest FOIA in the world) made high floor possible: the taxpayer sees where the money goes.",
  "constitution.lesson.transparency.title": "Elite transparency",
  "constitution.map.afterPrefix": "after:",
  "constitution.map.axesLegend": "X axis — elite fear of the bottom (← higher) · Y axis — innovation / drive (↑ higher)",
  "constitution.map.description": "The «you are here» point is calculated from the current sliders. The further right — the less elites fear the bottom. The higher — the more drive and innovation. Countries are an approximate estimate on a 0-100 scale; not a scientific ranking, but a tool for intuition.",
  "constitution.map.heading": "Where you stand on the world map",
